<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Victory Stela of Piye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 727 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Great Triumphal Stela of Piye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Victory Stela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piye">Piye</a> is a granite stela set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebel-barkal">Jebel Barkal</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> by the Kushite king Piye around 727 BCE. Its long hieroglyphic text describes his campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tefnakht">Tefnakht</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a> and the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a> and the Delta, including the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a>, and their submission. It is the main source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s political fragmentation at the time and is now in the Egyptian Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p></div>
